WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.292 --> 00:00:05.760
Speaker 1: Let's go next to. Oh, how about&nbsp;
Magnus from Norway? Magnus, you're on the air.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:13.520
Can you hear me? Just barely, Magnus, I'm going to&nbsp;
pump you up, pump up your volume as much as I can.&nbsp;

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:22.640
Speaker 7: Um, well, let me try first. Wow.
Speaker 1: You're barely audible.&nbsp;

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:31.040
Speaker 7: Yeah, wait a second here.&nbsp;
Uh. Why is it so low? OK. Is it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:35.440
possible to try again? A little bit later?
Speaker 1: Yeah. You know, if you speak up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:38.720
I've pumped you up 200 percent, so maybe&nbsp;
we can hear you if you have a question.&nbsp;

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:42.320
Speaker 7: Erm, yeah, I was just&nbsp;
wondering, um, what do you think about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:47.120
privatization of electricity?
Speaker 1: Privatization of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:51.120
electricity. So are you talking about&nbsp;
the generation or the distribution?&nbsp;

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:58.480
Speaker 7: Um, I guess you can say both.
Speaker 1: Hmm. I have no reason to think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:02.320
it's something we should do. Why would&nbsp;
we why would we want to do it, I guess.&nbsp;

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:09.200
Speaker 7: I know like the reason I'm I don't&nbsp;
know if I'm pro or anything but like, um.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:15.760
I feel like it will help in reducing the&nbsp;
price of electricity in homes and stuff&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:23.600
like, for instance, in Norway. We have had&nbsp;
some increase in the cost of electricity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:35.120
Yeah, because uh, as my mother says, she says&nbsp;
that it's because of the EU that we have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:43.680
increase the prices of like water, for instance.&nbsp;
Um, but I'm like, if if we just privatize it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:49.120
wouldn't it like decrease the prices?
Speaker 1: It's not obvious to me that it would,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:54.640
you know, I can only speak to the United States.&nbsp;
But in the United States, electricity is quite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.640 --> 00:02:00.800
regulated in different states have different&nbsp;
configurations, but one argument would be open&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:09.440
competition and then it will reduce the price. But&nbsp;
the other side of it would be actually it would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:15.520
actually increase the price once you make it a for&nbsp;
profit model. Now, in some states where there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:21.840
been deregulation, you can change your electricity&nbsp;
supplier, you can shop around. But the complexity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:27.200
with electricity is the delivery. The delivery is&nbsp;
still happening by one company because otherwise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:33.840
you need if you want to change power company,&nbsp;
you need different power lines, and that's very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:39.680
complicated to have each company run its own&nbsp;
power lines. So if you concede that the delivery&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:44.560
needs to be based on one infrastructure, you&nbsp;
then start to kind of have a little bit of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:50.400
question mark around what gain, what benefit there&nbsp;
really is to opening it up in the way that you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:55.200
suggesting. So I'm just not sure about that.
Speaker 7: Oh, thanks for answering it.&nbsp;

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:59.040
Speaker 1: My pleasure, Magnus from&nbsp;
Norway, thank you so much for the call.

